The scp command in Linux stands for Secure Copy Protocol.
It’s used to securely transfer files between computers over a network using SSH (Secure Shell).

-----How scp Works
It uses SSH for authentication and encryption, so your files are transferred securely.
You can copy files:
From local → remote
From remote → local
Between two remote hosts (via your local machine as a controller).

------Syntax
scp source destination

-----Copy file from local to remote
scp  file.txt  user@remote_host://home/destination_user_path

------Copy file from remote to local
scp user@remote_host:/home/user/file.txt  /local/directory/

-----Copy a whole directory
scp -r myfolder teja@192.168.1.50:/home/teja/
